<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Latest news from the Middle East, daily international news reports - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">FRANCE 24’s coverage and analyses of political and social issues across the Middle East, including Egypt, the Gulf states, Iraq, Israel, Jordan, Lebanon, Saudi Arabia, Syria, Yemen and beyond.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/rss</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Live: Saudi Arabia reports Aramco building hit and four wounded in Houthi missile attacks</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3e67c0ffe1d63f04123ecef5e032bf15ad85fb1570482e575e8516a6da55acfe</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-14T03:39:11Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261014-live-saudi-arabia-reports-aramco-building-hit-and-four-wounded-in-houthi-missile-attacks" /><summary type="html">Saudi Arabia reported damage to an Aramco building in Dhahran and four people wounded in Riyadh on Wednesday, after the kingdom intercepted Houthi missiles targeting the capital. Follow our liveblog for the latest updates.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Oil facility near Yemen capital Sanaa hit by Saudi Arabia, Houthis say</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f9dc5d531da35a9afadd999d97f454beeed00bcd83cb09ee34ddf5e58bbee4bb</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-13T14:53:39Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261013-oil-facility-near-yemen-capital-sanaa-hit-by-saudi-arabia-houthis-say--987287f5942b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261013-oil-facility-near-yemen-capital-sanaa-hit-by-saudi-arabia-houthis-say" /><summary type="html">Houthi media reported an ​attack ‌by Saudi Arabia on ⁠the ‌Yemen Oil Company ⁠in Sanaa on Tuesday, which came ‌shortly after the ‌Saudi-led coalition ​ordered the evacuation of the ​facility ​ahead of ​a military ​operation. The Iran-backed rebels had warned that they would target Riyadh's airport and other sites in the country if Saudi Arabia followed through on its threat.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>French embassy in Riyadh tells citizens to avoid Saudi airports targeted by Houthis</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e90d7d33dbfc944ebc177f087899ce6f137c29d0176886fdb55cffce4ee18d22</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-13T13:51:23Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261013-french-embassy-in-riyadh-tells-citizens-to-avoid-saudi-airports-targeted-by-houthis--8a1922457886.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261013-french-embassy-in-riyadh-tells-citizens-to-avoid-saudi-airports-targeted-by-houthis" /><summary type="html">France's embassy in Saudi Arabia told its citizens to avoid the airports in Riyadh, Jeddah and Dammam on Tuesday as fighting escalates between a Saudi-led coalition and Yemen's Iran-backed Houthi rebels, who have been attacking Saudi airports and oil facilities since last week.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>'Hope so heavy to carry': Al-Shaer on harrowing plight, emotional toll, struggle to survive in Gaza</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3b0ab5fbb29902b012187afe036343c02dc90e8921a88c4853092e36860555da</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-13T13:36:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/hope-so-heavy-to-carry-al-shaer-on-harrowing-plight-emotional-toll-struggle-to-survive-in-gaza--fdf8893cdd4c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/hope-so-heavy-to-carry-al-shaer-on-harrowing-plight-emotional-toll-struggle-to-survive-in-gaza" /><summary type="html">Eve Irvine is pleased to welcome Palestinian writer and poet Mahmoud Al-Shaer, “Am I real, or just a number in this war?” In I Am Still Alive: Dispatches from Gaza, Palestinian writer Mahmoud Al-Shaer explores how war transforms memory, childhood and the meaning of existence. Recounting his family's displacement, his daughter's understanding of a world reduced to tents, and his evacuation from Gaza, he reflects on writing as an act of resistance against erasure. His testimony reveals how the struggle for survival extends beyond physical destruction to the preservation of identity, collective memory and the possibility of a normal life.</summary></entry><entry><title>Airlines suspend flights to Riyadh amid security fears</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b27cbe92a8ede7519f9f88e1afbe8355b8ff3dc9c17075c78fe2dd51d3e8a4fa</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-13T09:19:23Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/airlines-suspend-flights-to-riyadh-amid-security-fears--7c139092e54b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/airlines-suspend-flights-to-riyadh-amid-security-fears" /><summary type="html">Flights to and from Riyadh have been suspended following deadly Houthi attacks, with several international airlines extending cancellations through October 19. The disruption threatens Saudi Arabia’s ambitions to become a major global aviation hub, while Delta has postponed its first direct US flights to the Kingdom until March 2027.</summary></entry><entry><title>No, these pictures do not show the Houthi attacks on Riyadh's King Khalid Airport</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c08be6f801575dd869a15e891c1936901073331674c7a699289bb397144d94db</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T20:59:18Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/truth-or-fake/20261012-no-these-pictures-do-not-show-the-houthi-attacks-on-riyadh-king-khalid-airport--5ddc7b88c7ae.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/truth-or-fake/20261012-no-these-pictures-do-not-show-the-houthi-attacks-on-riyadh-king-khalid-airport" /><summary type="html">As Saudi Arabia’s King Khalid International Airport suffers a string of deadly attacks, claimed by Yemen's Houthi rebels, a slew of AI-generated or decontextualised videos and photos have been circulating on social media, purporting to show the strikes of the last few days. We take a closer look in Truth or Fake.</summary><category term="Truth or Fake" /></entry><entry><title>Houthi official warns of 'swift, painful response' if US enters Yemen war</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:aaa7cb116a8c36830eae57681df292dce277b9860e43b6ec0bc681ffb5c41350</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T19:42:01Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/houthi-official-warns-of-swift-painful-response-if-us-enters-yemen-war--7b3f562a98c3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/houthi-official-warns-of-swift-painful-response-if-us-enters-yemen-war" /><summary type="html">A Houthi official on Monday warned the US it would receive a "painful response" if it entered the war in Yemen, where Saudi Arabia has reportedly requested Washington's help in dealing with the group as it weathers repeated attacks. FRANCE 24's Monte Francis reports.</summary></entry><entry><title>Houthis 'are thriving and surviving by creating war', Yemen ambassador says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:856668e4d6429a7ea8a04b2475011a3fdc4d99777394faf3a90e31ef1df10ad9</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T19:08:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/houthis-are-thriving-and-surviving-by-creating-war-yemen-ambassador-says--2600efc926ad.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/houthis-are-thriving-and-surviving-by-creating-war-yemen-ambassador-says" /><summary type="html">Speaking with FRANCE 24's Mark Owen, Yemen's ambassador to France Riyad Yassin Abdallah says that the Iran-backed Houthi "are thriving and surviving by creating war". Saudi Arabia's energy minister on Monday said the country had been tested but would mount a "serious" response after a string of attacks on Riyadh's international airport by Yemen's Houthis that left 18 people dead.</summary></entry><entry><title>How exposed is Saudi Arabia? Yemen's Houthis scale up drone and missile attacks</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1b86645d0ddceb28708ddd8467299895256745587e1cfe12862c08f7da2950f5</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T18:33:12Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261012-how-exposed-is-saudi-arabia-yemen-s-houthis-scale-up-drone-and-missile-attacks--9dcebaf3c58e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261012-how-exposed-is-saudi-arabia-yemen-s-houthis-scale-up-drone-and-missile-attacks" /><summary type="html">How exposed is Saudi Arabia? Just as Iran's retaliation to Donald Trump's war suddenly exposed the vulnerability of neighbouring Gulf states, how much is Riyadh reeling from the escalation with Yemen's Tehran-backed Houthis? We assess the physical and material damage from strikes on airports and oil facilities, as well as eventual repercussions for the kingdom's ability to project strength. In the age of cheap drone warfare, how much can a battle-hardened militia push against the region's richest nation?</summary><category term="The Debate" /></entry><entry><title>Saudi Arabia extends Riyadh aiport suspension, vows deadly response to Houthis strikes</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:26e03f61936333c908529cd4697196e1cebfef1707c0bfa54b27b3e0a548b5fd</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T16:21:57Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/saudi-arabia-extends-riyadh-aiport-suspension-vows-deadly-response-to-houthis-strikes--faba784fd28a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/saudi-arabia-extends-riyadh-aiport-suspension-vows-deadly-response-to-houthis-strikes" /><summary type="html">Saudi Arabia extended on Monday the closure of the international airport in Riyadh after announcing the Saudi-led coalition was responding to Houthi combined drone and missile attacks. On Saturday, another attack left 13 dead and over 300 injured. This is one of the worst disruptions to Saudi aviation in years – marking a significant escalation in the war with Yemen's Houthi rebels. Details and analysis by FRANCE 24 international affairs editor Philip Turle.</summary></entry><entry><title>Concerts of pro-Assad singers cancelled in Syria</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:89238b2095a7947d771b606b28ad4020c825452806af5e90d1b4874dc3ab3f08</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T13:53:58Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20261012-concerts-pro-assad-singers-cancelled--7f221a636658.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20261012-concerts-pro-assad-singers-cancelled" /><summary type="html">The concerts of several singers accused of supporting the regime of deposed president Bashar al-Assad have been cancelled this summer in Syria. People in Homs ripped down posters advertising performances by artists Mohamad Iskandar and Hossam Jneed, denouncing their previous links with the regime. One of the musicians later publicly apologised.</summary><category term="The Observers" /></entry><entry><title>'Perfect Victims' author Mohammed El-Kurd challenges how Palestinian suffering is viewed</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d1d483939ef553ae5af3ebf3095e2b1ff1da775256e8038837075ac5e21cfd42</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T13:12:47Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261012-perfect-victims-author-mohammed-el-kurd-challenges-how-palestinian-suffering-is-viewed--ba6b4c5c0ead.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261012-perfect-victims-author-mohammed-el-kurd-challenges-how-palestinian-suffering-is-viewed" /><summary type="html">Mohammed El-Kurd is a Palestinian writer, poet and journalist. He is the author of the poetry collection Rifqa and the book "Perfect Victims and the Politics of Appeal", which was just published in French translation last week. Mohammed grew up in Sheikh Jarrah in East Jerusalem and as a child, his family were displaced from their home by Israeli settlers in a high-profile eviction. He spoke to us in Perspective.</summary><category term="Perspective" /></entry><entry><title>Netanyahu demands probe into Israeli anonymous whistleblowers in 'NAZA' documentary</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:73bd665250936dae045e1694460478f50acd3742d2abd0d6f924c1caa45231ab</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T11:17:27Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/netanyahu-demands-probe-into-israeli-anonymous-whistleblowers-in-naza-documentary--d86bdd3a3ce6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/netanyahu-demands-probe-into-israeli-anonymous-whistleblowers-in-naza-documentary" /><summary type="html">Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu on Sunday called for an investigation into the anonymous sources featured in the award-winning Gaza war documentary "NAZA". The film, which won the Special Jury Prize at the Venice Film Festival last month, is based on testimony from 24 unnamed Israeli military whistleblowers who accuse the army of killing numerous civilians in Gaza with the use of AI-powered targeting systems. Details by FRANCE 24 correspondent in Jerusalem, Noga Tarnopolsky.</summary></entry><entry><title>Saudi Arabia: Riyadh airport struck again by Yemen's Houthis</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2fac1bd29187752f5123b44879402f3e9926e426ddd4fcb82b9e78f44ef86549</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T09:21:12Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/saudi-arabia-riyadh-airport-struck-again-by-yemen-s-houthis--7707c4a7af25.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/saudi-arabia-riyadh-airport-struck-again-by-yemen-s-houthis" /><summary type="html">A new attack hit Riyadh's international airport on Sunday, a day after another attack on the airport killed at least 12 people and injured over 300. This was the deadliest on Saudi soil in over a decade. Fighting between the Iran-backed Houthis and Saudi-backed Yemeni government forces have been escalating for several weeks. Story by Juliette Brown and Caitlin Brown.</summary></entry><entry><title>Riyadh airport to remain closed after deadly Houthi attacks</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4a5fbb48b79980052b1205e360f07696646d1ef587d7fed9b945e0a38868ec45</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T08:41:12Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261012-live-saudi-arabia-extends-suspension-of-operations-at-riyadh-airport-after-deadly-houthi-at--4fd2df84c583.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261012-live-saudi-arabia-extends-suspension-of-operations-at-riyadh-airport-after-deadly-houthi-attacks" /><summary type="html">Saudi Arabia's civil aviation body extended the suspension of operations at Riyadh's international airport Monday following a series of deadly attacks by Yemen's Iran-aligned Houthi rebels over the weekend. The statement announcing the decision did not specify when operations would resume. Follow our liveblog to see how the day's events unfolded.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Fresh Houthi strike hits Riyadh airport a day after deadly attack</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:aca592823ae347460a7dafcdd04ea397f57c395b0d9cadc58bb68e1418916aab</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T01:01:39Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261012-houthi-strike-riyadh-airport-yemen--400440c0ef77.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261012-houthi-strike-riyadh-airport-yemen" /><summary type="html">A fresh Houthi strike hit Riyadh’s King Khalid International Airport on Sunday, killing a Pakistani resident and wounding 11, Saudi authorities said. The attack followed a strike that killed at least 12 people and halted airport operations on Saturday. The Iran-backed group threatened further attacks on Saudi Arabia’s transit hubs.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Escalating fighting in Yemen worsens food security crisis</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:49d4fa047fb36636977cb58576b18ffd803d8e74cec99a4aedab3054cc4683a6</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-11T06:34:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/escalating-fighting-in-yemen-worsens-food-security-crisis--42dac36f429b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/escalating-fighting-in-yemen-worsens-food-security-crisis" /><summary type="html">Aid agencies in Yemen highlighted the growing hunger emergency sparked by renewed fighting between Houthi opposition forces and Government troops, after mobile health clinics reported that hundreds of children displaced by the violence are suffering from the most deadly form of malnutrition.</summary></entry><entry><title>Saudi Arabia threatens 'decisive' response after Riyadh airport strike kills at least 12</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0e88e2ec24ebdd0f058da8386fa84c65457b9fb50e58b399ce7ae29aaa485dd3</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-11T06:21:50Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261011-riyadh-airport-strike-kills-12-saudi-arabia-threatens-decisive-response--ddc088b6e762.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261011-riyadh-airport-strike-kills-12-saudi-arabia-threatens-decisive-response" /><summary type="html">Saudi Arabia vowed to retaliate against Yemen’s Houthi rebels on Sunday after a strike on Riyadh’s international airport killed a dozen people and wounded more than 300, marking the kingdom's deadliest strike in a decade.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Riyadh airport strike kills 12, Saudi Arabia threatens 'decisive' response</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:266e9eb0e171807624ebba650cdfc4a7432a69f546538098b54190903feeb050</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-11T05:16:22Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261011-at-least-twelve-killed-multiple-people-wounded-in-attack-on-riyadh-airport-1--0687f083d6fd.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261011-at-least-twelve-killed-multiple-people-wounded-in-attack-on-riyadh-airport-1" /><summary type="html">Saudi Arabia vowed to retaliate against Yemen’s Houthi rebels on Sunday after a strike on Riyadh’s international airport killed a dozen people and wounded more than 300, marking the kingdom's deadliest strike in a decade.</summary></entry><entry><title>Houthi rebels claim latest attacks on Riyadh airport, following Saturday's deadly strike</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:854b76f26ac1a5919f93e70faee453466cdd3dca0901f1dec00a51f831a5b59d</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-11T05:04:26Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261011-live-saudi-arabia-vows-decisive-response-after-deadly-houthi-strike-on-riyadh-airport--725a01a68080.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261011-live-saudi-arabia-vows-decisive-response-after-deadly-houthi-strike-on-riyadh-airport" /><summary type="html">Yemen's Houthis have claimed responsibility for attacks on Riyadh's international airport on Sunday, which the Saudi Civil Aviation Authority reported as having caused injuries. The attacks follow Saturday’s strike on the civilian airport which killed at least 12, constituting the deadliest on Saudi soil in more than a decade. Read our blog to see how the day's events unfolded.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Why Yemen's war is escalating</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c17e2bd4b9afd3bdb6aac105bf55692a9dbc89b6fcd2087b3b005f6ac962be0f</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-10T18:22:23Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261010-spotlight--311f91c483a9.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261010-spotlight" /><summary type="html">Loud explosions have been heard at Riyadh airport. Diplomatic sources say that Yemen's Houthi fighters have launched another missile attack on the international hub. At least five people have been treated for extensive injuries, according to hospital sources, with dozens more injured. The Saudi government hasn't yet commented, as more airlines cancel flights to the country. Meanwhile, Saudi jets continue to strike Yemen, in support of the Yemeni government involved in fierce fighting to push back Houthi positions.</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>At least twelve killed, multiple people wounded in attack on Riyadh airport</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f74e5999decc86fdf06805c34fd6d28862a371a3fd9e9165b8b20fc0a891d3f9</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-10T18:03:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261010-riyadh-airport-suspends-operations-after-attack-leaves-multiple-people-wounded--adcd0c7fcd1d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261010-riyadh-airport-suspends-operations-after-attack-leaves-multiple-people-wounded" /><summary type="html">At least twelve people were killed in an attack on Riyadh's King Khalid International Airport on Saturday, including an Egyptian flight attendant, according to a medical source at a local hospital. Earlier this week, another attack on the airport by the Houthis killed three people and damaged a plane, though the Iran-backed group did not announce any new attacks on Saturday.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Yemen says advancing against Houthis around key Bab al-Mandab strait</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:43c5ea7c1cf8e0f144c944bf7ebcce63abc101726d7e2a915f9c050eb9e78991</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-10T13:45:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261010-yemen-says-forces-made-advances-against-houthis-around-key-waterway--4e2af6409b56.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261010-yemen-says-forces-made-advances-against-houthis-around-key-waterway" /><summary type="html">Yemen said Saturday that forces were advancing against the Houthis around the Bab al-Mandab strait, a week after the country's Saudi-backed government announced that it was initiating a counter-offensive to take back territory seized by the pro-Iran group during last month's lightning offensive. In Riyadh, diplomatic sources reported a missile attack on the civilian airport, with at least five Saudi nationals wounded being treated in intensive care.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Palestinian president Abbas postpones legislative, presidential elections to September 2027</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a4ceae9a53ad9fb95c73d9edebc095ed240707cff33853598dd7ca4b1994cfcf</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-10T11:56:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261010-palestinian-president-abbas-postpones-legislative-elections-to-september-2027--79eb133759e7.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261010-palestinian-president-abbas-postpones-legislative-elections-to-september-2027" /><summary type="html">⁠⁠Palestinian president ​Mahmoud ​Abbas announced on Saturday that legislative ⁠elections would be postponed from November ​28 to September ​11, 2027, the ​official Palestinian news agency ⁠WAFA reported. In his presidential decree, Abbas set the next presidential elections for that day as well.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Palestinians struggle to find food in Gaza a year after ceasefire</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c1eadfe06cef7a036d20faf0742010b97945b292eb2a0a6e3b85037ee2bfe954</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-10T04:31:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261010-hunger-and-malnutrition-stalk-gazans-a-year-on-from-ceasefire--12017f60e848.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261010-hunger-and-malnutrition-stalk-gazans-a-year-on-from-ceasefire" /><summary type="html">Widespread hunger remains persistent in the Gaza Strip despite the ceasefire agreement between Hamas and Israel a year ago that was supposed to allow more food into the Palestinian territory. A new study published on Thursday by Médecins du Monde reported that 93 percent of pregnant or breastfeeding women had symptoms of anaemia, which is closely linked to malnutrition.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>France protests, Israel's 'politically charged' remembrance, Ethiopia, Ukraine</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fb97a0c13b0220c2064bc93ef5fe57d963e9e4f8fec959781cb0d64e2fb8682a</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-09T18:28:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-world-this-week/20261009-france-protests-israel-s-politically-charged-remembrance-ethopia-ukraine--45fe531857a1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-world-this-week/20261009-france-protests-israel-s-politically-charged-remembrance-ethopia-ukraine" /><summary type="html">It’s been a week of vigils, protests and political upheaval – from Israel, where three years after the Hamas-led attacks, former hostages and mourners called for accountability over the failures of October 7, as questions grow over the stalled path to peace in Gaza; to France, where mass demonstrations over chronic underfunding and poor conditions have drawn students, unions and hundreds of thousands of protesters into the streets, just months ahead of a presidential election. In Spain, Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez has made the housing crisis the centrepiece of a high-stakes electoral gamble, as protests intensify following the death of a pensioner whose eviction helped ignite the movement. And in the UK, questions remain over a suspected Iranian plot targeting US aircraft at RAF Fairford, after American B-1 bombers were hastily withdrawn amid warnings of a serious and immediate threat.</summary><category term="The World This Week" /></entry><entry><title>FlyDubai co-pilot planned suicide attack on Tel Aviv airport inspired by 9/11, prosecutor says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:98377facfcf29db76fcda40902c7c0c0385eaabb64af88096e84c325b8c27fff</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-09T17:04:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261009-flydubai-co-pilot-planned-suicide-attack-on-tel-aviv-airport-inspired-by-9-11--e457237ce44f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261009-flydubai-co-pilot-planned-suicide-attack-on-tel-aviv-airport-inspired-by-9-11" /><summary type="html">The Omani co-pilot who attacked the captain of FlyDubai flight 1073 had planned to crash the plane into Tel Aviv's international airport in a suicide attack, the United Arab Emirates' top prosecutor said Friday. The Israel-bound flight carrying 182 people made an emergency landing in Saudi Arabia after a descent so extreme that part of its rudder was torn away.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Palestinians gained 'nothing' from October 7, Egyptian businessman Naguib Sawiris says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7325d9d8073acf03c241e4ae37156faa25c38333b6da310fb396ee261af063cf</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-08T11:12:58Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t_te-_-t_te/20261008-palestinians-gained-nothing-from-october-7-egyptian-businessman-naguib-sawiris-says--ebd948c43a7c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20261008-palestinians-gained-nothing-from-october-7-egyptian-businessman-naguib-sawiris-says" /><summary type="html">In an interview with FRANCE 24, Egyptian businessman Naguib Sawiris called the October 7 attacks a "horrible act of terrorism" that brought Palestinians "only destruction", while warning of an "expansionist" Israeli government. He also said that the economic fallout from the Iran war is already "happening", defended doubling his UAE investment despite the regional conflict, and praised Syria's new president.</summary><category term="Tête à tête" /></entry><entry><title>Strait of Hormuz crisis fuels Cairo's plastic recycling business</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:14415af79d6baf03220ffa3db7ce90c28b84dc67cacc41a580c6f7686dbf007f</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-08T09:44:31Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261008-strait-of-hormuz-crisis-fuels-cairo-s-plastic-recycling-business--f882feb0c766.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261008-strait-of-hormuz-crisis-fuels-cairo-s-plastic-recycling-business" /><summary type="html">In the heart of the Egyptian capital Cairo, within the labyrinthine streets of the Manshiyet Nasser district, nearly 120,000 residents process a third of the megacity's waste every day. Long marginalised, these informal recycling specialists are now undergoing a dramatic transformation due to geopolitical tensions in the Strait of Hormuz, through which polyethylene from the Gulf usually passes.</summary><category term="Focus" /></entry><entry><title>Diplomats take shelter in Saudi capital as Yemen's Houthis target Riyadh airport</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:eac428c316a16918f9602e0620c12e23df841104fdc7774611ff5026a32896f0</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-08T09:20:46Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261008-diplomats-school-pupils-take-shelter-in-saudi-capital-as-yemen-s-houthis-step-up-attacks--02a4c212cf4f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261008-diplomats-school-pupils-take-shelter-in-saudi-capital-as-yemen-s-houthis-step-up-attacks" /><summary type="html">Foreign diplomats and international schools in the Saudi capital took safety measures on Thursday as loud blasts rattled the city and Yemen's Houthi rebels claimed missile attacks against its airport. In a further blow to Riyadh, Turkey ruled out sending troops to confront the Iran-backed militia, despite a defence pact with Saudi Arabia.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry></feed>
